# Desk 22 Oct 2026: Codex builds for the Opus engineering-correctness audit (areas/AUDIT_ENGINEERING_CIVIL_2026-10-22.md): the findings that are plain defects or already ruled by Tim. Items needing Tim's engineering word (rainfall curve numbers, pipe-wall cover, obvert rule, minimum grade table) are NOT built: they are in QUESTIONS_FOR_MONDAY.md.
$dir = 'C:\Lisp Files\_CODEX_RUNNER'
$enc = New-Object Text.UTF8Encoding($false)
$b1 = [IO.File]::ReadAllText("$dir\cx_b01_automation_ids.txt")
$pre = $b1.Substring(0, $b1.IndexOf('TASK:'))
$common = "SOURCE OF THE FINDINGS: C:\CE_PLAN\areas\AUDIT_ENGINEERING_CIVIL_2026-10-22.md (Opus engineering-correctness audit; read the finding named below with its file:line, its worked numeric example and its test). Each fix needs: unit tests using the audit's worked numbers (fail before, pass after), a REGRESSION_LOG.md entry, and an honest statement in the PR of every engineering assumption you made. Tim is a civil engineer: wrong numbers on an issued drainage design are the harm; where the audit marks a point 'suspected' or 'not certain', verify it in the code first and say so. If a fix would change a number a drafter already issued, state it plainly in the PR."
$b = @(
 @('cx_b06_eng_small', "TASK: FOUR SMALL CERTAIN FIXES from the engineering audit. (a) Finding 16: the red back-test BackTest_GlenEira24115_Computed_WithinTolerance is out of date, not the engine: hold the issued sheet's soft-ground C 0.35 and its 5.87 L/s permitted discharge in the test the same way the GE 23-129 test holds its soft-ground C, keep the 30 percent band, and state the new percentage error; explain GE 23-129 passing at 28.6 percent. (b) Finding 23: the CDDESIGN window shows the council's soft C (0.35) while the calculation uses the Settings value 0.30: make the window show the value actually used. (c) Finding 21: the R-03 message can contradict itself after rounding (20.04 m over 0.10 m is 1 in 200.4 but reads 'actual 1:200 is flatter than the ladder minimum 1:200'): make the message consistent (compare and print the same rounded values or print one decimal). (d) Finding 19: Casey's formula uses Math.Round with no mode (banker's rounding): use MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero if the engineering sheet rounds halves up (check the issued sheet logic and the audit) and add the exact-half test. Branch cx/eng-small-c60. Report C:\CE_PLAN\areas\CODEX_BUILD_ENG_SMALL_22OCT.md."),
 @('cx_b07_real_length', "TASK: FINDING 1 (HIGH): CDRULES and CDCHECKS work grade and speed on the straight pit-centre distance, not the drawn route or the real pipe length (example: a 225 drawn at 1 in 240 passes as 1 in 170 where it should warn against the 1 in 200 minimum). Fix so the grade and speed checks use the real length of the pipe object (the drawn route, including bends and same-plan breaks) where CE has it, falling back to the straight distance only when it does not, with a plain note in the result row saying which was used. Settings-switch rule: the old behaviour is the bug, so no switch, but the fallback must be logged. Tests with the audit's worked numbers, including a pipe with a bend. Branch cx/eng-real-length-c60. Report C:\CE_PLAN\areas\CODEX_BUILD_ENG_REAL_LENGTH_22OCT.md."),
 @('cx_b08_pipe_material', "TASK: FINDING 2 (HIGH): CDRUNSOLVE, the network redesign and the proposals that write sizes treat every pipe as UPVC, so on concrete pipe capacity is over-stated by about 23 percent (a 375 at 1 in 130 is sized for 200 L/s but carries 154 L/s). Find where the pipe material/roughness is stored (pipe class, Manning n or Colebrook k in Settings) and make capacity, sizing and the proposals use the pipe's own material, with the material shown in the proposal text. Where material is Unknown keep the current default but say so in the row (Unknown stays Unknown rule: never silently assume). Tests with the audit's numbers (375 at 1 in 130 concrete 154 L/s, UPVC 200 L/s). Branch cx/eng-pipe-material-c60. Report C:\CE_PLAN\areas\CODEX_BUILD_ENG_PIPE_MATERIAL_22OCT.md."),
 @('cx_b09_pit_floor_depth', "TASK: FINDING 8 (Tim's ruling 20 Oct item 13: pit depth means the PIT FLOOR). The Pit Schedule still measures depth to the outlet pipe. Verify first whether the pit record holds its floor (InvertLevel or a pit-floor field) and say so; then make the Pit Schedule (window, drawn schedule, CDSYNC compare, exports) use the pit floor when the pit has one and the outlet pipe invert otherwise, label the column honestly, and keep every other number unchanged. Settings-switch: the old behaviour is the defect (Tim ruled), so default ON with the reason in the PR; the drawn schedule on an existing drawing changes only after the drafter re-syncs. Tests with the audit's example. Branch cx/eng-pit-floor-c60. Report C:\CE_PLAN\areas\CODEX_BUILD_ENG_PIT_FLOOR_22OCT.md."),
 @('cx_b10_move_pit_grade', "TASK: FINDING 6 (MEDIUM): moving a pit with a held grade rounds the bottom level to the NEAREST step, so the pipe can come out flatter than the grade it was held at. Make it round in the safe direction exactly as MathHelper CD calculated levels already do (drop rounded UP, never flatter than the chosen grade; see the audit's 'Checked and found sound' item 5 and LevelsProposalEngine). Tests with the audit's numbers, including a case that was flatter before. Branch cx/eng-move-pit-grade-c60. Report C:\CE_PLAN\areas\CODEX_BUILD_ENG_MOVE_PIT_22OCT.md."),
 @('cx_b11_twl_units', "TASK: FINDING 10 (MEDIUM): CDTWL ignores the drawing's units when it measures pipes (a metre drawing gives lengths 1000 times wrong, so the storage volume and freeboard are wrong). Use the same drawing-unit conversion as the rest of the Civil code (INSUNITS 4 mm, 6 m, 0 treated as the existing default with the soft note decided for BUG-505: do not add the note here), apply it wherever CDTWL reads a length or a level, and add tests for a millimetre and a metre drawing with identical physical geometry giving identical volumes. Also FINDING 9 only if it is a clear double count you can prove from the code with a unit test; otherwise leave it and say so. Branch cx/eng-twl-units-c60. Report C:\CE_PLAN\areas\CODEX_BUILD_ENG_TWL_UNITS_22OCT.md.")
)
foreach ($x in $b) { [IO.File]::WriteAllText("$dir\$($x[0]).txt", $pre + $common + "`n" + $x[1] + "`n", $enc) }
$wt = 'C:/Lisp Files/_CODEX_RUNNER'
function L($n) { "$n|$wt|gpt-6.1-sol|high|98|6" }
$l9 = @((L 'cx_b07_real_length'), (L 'cx_b08_pipe_material'), (L 'cx_b10_move_pit_grade'))
